The OpenMP vendor string in numba.np.ufunc.omppool is chosen at compile time
(__clang__ -> "Intel", which icx/icpx define; otherwise "GNU" on Linux).
When numba is built with Intel oneAPI icx/icpx on Linux, the omppool backend is
correctly linked against Intel OpenMP (libiomp5) and reports "Intel", but the
test hard-codes "GNU" for Linux. Accept both vendors on Linux.
Applies unchanged to numba 0.60.0 and 0.62.0.
Author: Simon Pinches (ITER)
diff -ruN numba-0.60.0.orig/numba/tests/test_parallel_backend.py numba-0.60.0/numba/tests/test_parallel_backend.py
--- numba-0.60.0.orig/numba/tests/test_parallel_backend.py	2024-06-13 10:56:33.000000000 +0200
+++ numba-0.60.0/numba/tests/test_parallel_backend.py	2026-09-06 11:33:56.538988412 +0200
@@ -1238,15 +1238,16 @@
         Checks the OpenMP vendor strings are correct
         """
         expected = dict()
-        expected['win32'] = "MS"
-        expected['darwin'] = "Intel"
-        expected['linux'] = "GNU"
+        expected['win32'] = ("MS",)
+        expected['darwin'] = ("Intel",)
+        # Intel oneAPI (icx/icpx) uses Intel OpenMP (libiomp5) on Linux too
+        expected['linux'] = ("GNU", "Intel")
 
         # only check OS that are supported, custom toolchains may well work as
         # may other OS
         for k in expected.keys():
             if sys.platform.startswith(k):
-                self.assertEqual(expected[k], omppool.openmp_vendor)
+                self.assertIn(omppool.openmp_vendor, expected[k])
 
 
 if __name__ == '__main__':
